WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.542
By the end of this module, you should be able to choose a join type based on the business question,

00:00:05.542 --> 00:00:10.107
not simply because one join looks familiar.

00:00:10.107 --> 00:00:16.822
When someone says, show me only customers who placed an order, that suggests one type of join.

00:00:16.822 --> 00:00:22.092
When they say, show me every customer, including those with no orders, that suggests another.

00:00:22.092 --> 00:00:29.452
SQL becomes easier when you translate the wording of the question into a rule about which rows must remain.

00:00:29.452 --> 00:00:34.382
You will also practice using table aliases.

00:00:34.382 --> 00:00:40.832
When two or three tables contain columns with similar names, aliases keep the query readable.

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:45.072
Instead of wondering which patient_id we mean, we can make the source explicit.

00:00:45.072 --> 00:00:55.012
You should also become comfortable reading a join from left to right and explaining each table's role in plain

00:00:55.012 --> 00:00:58.357
language. A major goal is understanding grain.

00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:01.142
Grain means what one row represents.

00:01:01.142 --> 00:01:06.477
One row might represent one patient, one admission, one lab test, one order, or one department.

00:01:06.477 --> 00:01:14.382
If you expect one row per patient but your query returns ten rows for one patient, that may be a warning sign.

00:01:14.382 --> 00:01:19.052
On the other hand, if the result is supposed to be one row per admission,

00:01:19.052 --> 00:01:23.672
repeated patient names may be completely correct.

00:01:23.672 --> 00:01:26.057
Finally, you should be able to validate a join.

00:01:26.057 --> 00:01:32.542
We will compare row counts, distinct IDs, unmatched records, and known examples.

00:01:32.542 --> 00:01:36.117
In real projects, validation is not optional.

00:01:36.117 --> 00:01:43.802
A join that looks visually reasonable can still produce inflated counts or silently remove important records.

00:01:43.802 --> 00:01:48.662
By the end, I want you to be able to defend not only the SQL syntax,

00:01:48.662 --> 00:01:54.783
but also why the result represents the population and grain that the question actually requires.
